kind: instructions
title: "Lesson and assessment — the teaching composition and the item bank over one concept page"
summary: "A lesson carries the worked example and the curricular order for one wiki concept; its assessment carries the frozen items. Neither is a second copy of the page."
---

`context/learning/<concept-id>.md` is the **lesson** for one wiki concept;
`context/learning/<concept-id>.assessment.md` is its item bank. Both are
documents, not a runtime: `sdt learn lesson` composes the lesson and
`sdt learn record` alone writes learner evidence. Keep the key out of learner views.

Read it with `instructions/wiki.md` (the page it teaches) and
`instructions/learning.md` (how a session runs). This module covers the artefact
only.

## What a lesson is

- **One concept, one lesson, the same id.** Its `id` and `concept` equal the wiki
  page id (for example, `architecture/four-key-metrics`).
- **The wiki owns pure concept knowledge.** Objectives, difficulty and pacing
  live in the lesson frontmatter:

  ```yaml
  objectives:
    - "name the four metrics and the pipeline point each is measured at"
    - "calculate a metric over its reporting window"
  difficulty: intermediate   # introductory | intermediate | advanced
  minutes: 12                # 5-60, the review time
  privacy: none              # none | personal | real
  ```

  Every lesson and assessment declares `privacy: none|personal|real`. For `personal` or `real`,
  add `privacy_clearance: {status: cleared, basis: "short review basis"}`. A package refuses
  an undeclared value or a sensitive value without clearance; it never assumes `none`.

  Items point to objectives by 1-based index; preserve their order.
- **Create with `context new --type lesson`.** It writes `kind`, `uid`, `id`,
  `concept`, `summary`, `status`, timestamps, `project` and wiki links/sources.
  Lesson status is lifecycle; `sdt learn check` judges teachability.
- **A lesson is not a page.** No claim is introduced here. Every item names the
  claim it rests on (`<page-id>#claim-N`) and every example names the span it was
  re-expressed from.

## Sections, in this order and no others

`## Prerequisites` · `## Worked example` · `## Transfer`

- **Prerequisites** — what to learn first and why. This curricular order differs
  from logical `relations.depends_on`.
- **Worked example** — one solved example with `source_span` in
  `refs/<file>@<sha>:<start>-<end>` (line offsets). Cite and re-express; do not
  paste the source. Numbers omitted from the page may appear here. Quote only one
  short clause when exact wording matters, with its span (user decision,
  2026-10-28).
- **Transfer** — one unseen case without its solution. Items and keys stay in the
  assessment, not the learner-facing page.

## The assessment document

The assessment uses `kind: assessment`, id `<concept-id>.assessment`, its
`concept`, title/summary/status/timestamps/project and wiki links/sources; create
it with `context new --type assessment --slug <concept-id>`. It may precede a
lesson: quiz, answer and record can read it, while `learn check` requires a lesson.

Its only section is `## Items`. `key_hash` is the digest of what decides a verdict
— `expected`, `rubric`, `grading` and `distractor`; for a choice interaction it
also includes `interaction`, `options` and `scoring` — never the prompt, so a key
edit moves it and a wording edit does not. **Never compute it by hand**:
`sdt learn lesson <concept> --format json` carries each row's `key_digest`, the
value the cell holds. A cell that disagrees with its row is refused on read
(quiz, answer, record) and reported by `sdt context lint`.

## The item table

```markdown
| id | objective | type | prompt | expected | rubric | distractor | claim | grading | provenance | frozen | hash | key_hash | interaction | options | scoring | explanation_prompt |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| four-key-metrics-1 | 1 | recall | Name the four metrics. | deployment frequency; lead time for changes; change failure rate; time to restore service | all four, no extras | the four are individual performance targets | architecture/four-key-metrics#c-ab12cd34 | exact | material | 2026-10-28 | 4af036062f63 | 9f1c2d7a4b06 |  |  |  |  |
```

The four optional trailing columns enable portable QTI choice interactions.
`interaction` is `single-choice`, `multiple-choice`, `true-false` or
`choice-explanation`; `options` is a JSON array of `{ "id": "stable-id",
"text": "Option text" }`; `expected` is a JSON array of the correct option ids.
IDs are unique within the item and remain stable across wording edits. `scoring`
defaults to `exact-set`; `partial-credit` is only valid for multiple choice and
scores `(correct selected - incorrect selected) / correct option count`, clamped
to 0..1. `choice-explanation` adds an ungraded free-text response using
`explanation_prompt`; it does not alter the choice key. Rows with these columns
empty keep their existing free-answer grading semantics.

Export a frozen choice bank with `sdt learn export-qti <concept> > bank.zip`.
The package carries assessment content only, never learner identity or attempts.

- **`objective`** is 1-based against the lesson's `objectives`; every
  objective needs at least one item, and an index outside the list is a defect.
- **`id`** is unique and stable; renaming it orphans attempts.
- **`prompt`** asks the question and carries no part of the answer; **`rubric`**
  says what a complete answer shows; **`grading`** is `auto`, `exact`, `numeric`,
  `keywords` or `manual` (`auto` picks from the item's shape); **`provenance`** is
  `material`, `ai_generated`, `mixed` or `unknown`; **`frozen`** is the day the key
  was written.
- **`claim`** is `<page-id>#c-<id>` without the `wiki/` prefix. It must support
  the expected answer; positional `#claim-N` is reported until migrated.
- **`hash`** pins the claim text digest; read it from `sdt learn lesson --format
  json` or its text output. Validation reports `stale`, `unverified` (recommended
  to clear before shipping) or `broken` when the claim is gone.
- **`type`** is `recall`, `diagnose`, `apply` or `transfer`, an ordered ladder
  separate from page difficulty. An `apply` may use the worked example; say so in
  its rubric.
- **`expected`** is the answer, not a hint. For numeric answers, state unit,
  rounding and tolerance. Mark unsupported answers **not in the corpus**; derive
  keys from the page, never from a worked-example span.
- **`distractor`** is a real wrong model — the page's counter-case or a
  boundary — never a random option, and never the expected answer.
- Include a `transfer` item for an unseen case. The `## Transfer` section poses
  open work; the item makes it assessable.

## Check before you ship

```bash
sdt learn lesson <concept>              # the composed bundle
sdt learn lesson --validate             # every lesson: claim anchors, objective coverage, shape
sdt learn lesson --coverage             # objective → item matrix
sdt learn check <concept>               # is this concept teachable, and if not why
```

`sdt learn check` refuses a concept that cannot carry a complete lesson — page not
`active`, fewer than four claims, `maturity: wip`, no lesson objectives, no lesson,
or an objective without an item. It judges both the **page** and lesson metadata;
a lesson's own `status` is its lifecycle
and is not part of the verdict. A **missing `verified_at` is not a refusal**: a
stamp is provenance, not validity, and it is reported as a note. `--verified-only`
filters on it when a caller wants a freshness subset.

## Boundaries

- One lesson per concept; a second lesson for the same page is a split signal, not
  a variant.
- No answers stored in the page or lesson, and no learner state in either:
  `context/learning/learner.yaml` holds what a learner demonstrated.
- Never promote a page to `active` because a lesson needs it. The page is verified
  against its spans first (`wiki.md`), and the lesson follows.
